#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124344 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "8914449a-3d6f-5847-9e94-3be0a2ccbc94" :question/math-academy-id "e-9178" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $\\mathbf{q}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector\n2. $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}$\n3. $\\mathbf{q}$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector such that $P\\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{q}$. So, it's a steady-state vector of $P$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124345 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "d8798f55-f005-5019-bf47-349e43237888" :question/math-academy-id "e-9179" :question/problem "The table below describes how the customers of a particular electronics store feel about their next computer purchase.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Laptop** | **Desktop** |\n| To: | **Laptop** | $0.9$ | $0.8$ |\n| **Desktop** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nIt is found that $10 \\%$ of customers who currently use a laptop would choose a desktop on their next purchase, while $80 \\%$ of customers who currently use a desktop would choose a laptop on their next purchase.\n\nGiven that the store has $5\\,400$ customers, all of whom purchase a new computer every two years, how many customers would eventually own a desktop computer if the given probabilities remain constant over many two-year periods?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many two-year periods, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P$, and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that\n\n$$\nPq = q⟹(P - I)q = 0\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_{1} = 8x_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}8x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x_{2} + x_{2} &= 1 \\\\\n9x_{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx_{2} &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{9}}}\n$$\n\nof the $5\\,400$ customers would eventually own a desktop computer. Therefore, the total number of people who would eventually own a desktop computer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\cdot 5\\,400 = 600\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2164d0ba-43d5-53c7-adf1-9e6810b316a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9180" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the steady-state vector $\\mathbf q$ of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPq &= q \\\\\nPq - q &= 0 \\\\\nPq - Iq &= 0 \\\\\n(P - I)q &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation\n\n$$\n(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=20R_{1} \\\\ R_{2}:=10R_{2} \\\\ R_{3}:=20R_{3} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}↔R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 9R_{1} \\\\ R_{3}:=R_{3} - 7R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 57 & -19 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2x_1 -7\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\\right) + x_3 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = \\dfrac{2}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}x_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{3}x_{3} + \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} + x_{3} &= 1 \\\\\n2x_{3} &= 1 \\\\\nx_{3} &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "432478a8-bad6-5f9f-a515-4b0cf66d97e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121148} {:answer/id #uuid "52af6a52-6f24-59d0-a860-07c22969f81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121149} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0cde97-4842-5f30-a5d4-d6572633263c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121150} {:answer/id #uuid "63574b9e-c5f6-527e-8f99-288e3a17bc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121151} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8fe656-cd56-533b-9eda-08ac3657a873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63574b9e-c5f6-527e-8f99-288e3a17bc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121151} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5c9b48-0796-5549-a9b4-9a879bcd30f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121147}] :question/id #uuid "3662430d-84f0-5632-b066-9a7b273fafeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110189" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $q$ is a stochastic vector\n2. $Pq = q$\n3. $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82b4c7a0-e195-5c5e-919a-3112031d5ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121154} {:answer/id #uuid "9020e449-fe74-5614-836e-b46ada002820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121155} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ad6b11-a781-53e8-9f82-2f95ace10781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121156} {:answer/id #uuid "13dd5d4a-536a-54a9-88b5-c10652537dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121157} {:answer/id #uuid "3199d054-4549-5945-b935-7f80fa13239e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3199d054-4549-5945-b935-7f80fa13239e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121158} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b80cc76-1931-5799-8618-9b5bc1056106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121153}] :question/id #uuid "5d2ea1fa-42c3-5215-bede-8b745015a967" :question/math-academy-id "q-110239" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $q$ is a stochastic vector\n2. $Pq = q$\n3. $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8393f4e-aea7-56fc-b212-cf8c98502cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121160} {:answer/id #uuid "af5dda9e-fd3b-5334-82b6-2b657a97aa53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121161} {:answer/id #uuid "e24438a0-da2a-5e68-aade-96f44f597920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121162} {:answer/id #uuid "15b7850b-fe3a-51fd-b0da-e97fd832fffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121163} {:answer/id #uuid "72e5e789-03fc-5c09-b2f6-519cdc03a1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8393f4e-aea7-56fc-b212-cf8c98502cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121160} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d51feb7-ac17-54bd-b65a-a4fa5fc15ed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121159}] :question/id #uuid "496d0736-5684-529d-8dd0-8b061c9a6089" :question/math-academy-id "q-110242" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$, what is the value of $a - b$?"}]}
